 `replan request --run RUN` (`replan.request`) asks the runtime to replan a long-running run: only the person and the inbox. `replan propose --run RUN` (`replan.propose`) is a worker's proposal on its own run only, through the queue service in client mode; it stops and changes nothing. Parking a run and applying its replacement (`replan.park`, `replan.apply`) are the supervisor's alone, with no command. While a run's replanning holds its task, `ready`, `edit`, `cancel`, dependency changes, `integrate` and a resume of it are refused for every role, the person's included.
 
-`hold TASK|RUN --reason '...'` and `release TASK|RUN` (`task.hold`; a run's ID names its task) hold a task so that the runtime neither claims it nor resumes or recovers its run, and let it go: only the person and the inbox. A held task keeps its status, and a turn already going is not stopped. `release` is refused (`hold_not_settled`) until the run waits; `--provider` and `--model` set the task's later turns and runs. A run that goes to another provider, has no session, or stopped ends there, and the next run carries its branch and its uncommitted work (saved as a commit) over.
+`hold TASK|RUN --reason '...'` and `release TASK|RUN` (`task.hold`; a run's ID names its task) hold a task so that the runtime neither claims it nor resumes or recovers its run, and let it go: only the person and the inbox. A held task keeps its status, and a turn already going is not stopped. `release` is refused (`hold_not_settled`) until the run waits; `--provider` and `--model` set the task's later turns and runs. A stopped run (`failed`, `interrupted`) whose recovery job's verdict or answer the hold kept goes on by that result (resumes), session or not, when `release` gives neither `--provider` nor `--model`; with either of them, or with nothing kept, it ends. A run that waits (not stopped, such as `needs_session`) resumes in its session on the same provider, and ends when it has no session or goes to another provider. A run that ends leaves its branch and its uncommitted work (saved as a commit) to the next run.
 
 `setup-review` (`review.setup`) has a coding agent generate the review's settings in a temporary checkout and writes them to the working tree only at the person's approval (`reference/review-setup.md`): only the person and the inbox. Approving beforehand with `--yes` (`review.setup_approve`) is the person's alone: the inbox and every other actor are refused it. Its job (`setup-review-job`) may only read the queue: every command that changes state is refused.
 
